  "content": {
    "title": "USA-Dominican Republic WBC thriller ends on blatantly incorrect strikeout",
    "text": "Updated March 16, 2026, 10:20 a.m. ETThere was no helmet to tap, no modern technology to swoop in and keep alive the Dominican Republic’s hopes in the bottom of the ninth inning.Just a good old-fashioned blown call that the most passionate team in this World Baseball Classic might be stewing about for ages.With the tying run at third and Team USA closer Mason Miller clinging to a 2-1 lead in this World Baseball Classic semifinal, No. 9 hitter Geraldo Perdomo milked eight pitches out of the All-Star closer, his 102 mph fastball beginning to tick down a bit.So Miller threw a slider on that eighth pitch, an offering he failed to land earlier in the inning on several occasions. And this one, according to pitch-tracking systems, tumbled far below the bottom of the strike zone as well.Perdomo prepared to toss his bat and jog to first. The lineup would turn over, two runners on for leadoff man Fernando Tatís Jr., Miller’s pitch count soaring to 22.Home plate umpire Cory Blaser had a different outcome in mind: Game over.He rang up Perdomo, Team USA exulted and the Dominicans – who came into this semifinal averaging 10 runs per game – were left to wonder what might have happened had the game continued.Buy World Baseball Classic tickets!\"I knew it was 100% a ball,\" Perdomo told ESPN.It was the second time in three innings Blaser rang up a Dominican batter on a slider well below the zone. Juan Soto, one of the game’s most disciplined hitters, was caught looking at a Garrett Whitlock slider leading off the eighth. Replays showed it below his knees; pitch tracking technology confirmed it.Blaser is typically among the more reliable home plate umpires; his 94% accuracy rate in 2025 was in the upper half of all umps, though he's a tier down from the elite. And were this a Major League Baseball game in this, the year 2026, the Dominicans could have challenged the call with a simple tap of the helmet with the automatic ball-strike appeal system making its debut, assuming they had challenges remaining.Indeed, a Japanese umpire-tracking service based on the accurate and popular Umpire Scorecards showed Blaser called an excellent game overall, at 96.4% accuracy. Yet the two most egregious calls were, you guessed it, against Perdomo and Soto, providing Team USA a 0.64 runs advantage.It left the Dominicans in an odd spot of fuming at their missed chances yet aiming to respect the baseball theater that played out at Miami's loanDepot Park.\"I don't want to focus on the last pitch,\" Dominican manager Albert Pujols said in his postgame press conference. \"I'm not going to criticize any of that. It wasn't meant to be.\"Certainly, Team USA's pitching had plenty to do with it. The Dominicans came in averaging 10.3 runs in five games and hit a WBC-leading 14 homer - twice Team USA's seven. Yet Soto grounded into a bases-loaded double play against reliever Tyler Rogers in the fifth and their lone run came on Junior Caminero's solo homer against a typically strong Paul Skenes.The Dominicans did expend Skenes' pitch count by the fifth and threatened in the seventh against David Bednar and Miller in the ninth. Which only made the questionable calls sting even more.\"It was the whole tournament, but we didn't lose the game right there,\" Perdomo told ESPN. \"That's a part of the game, and I hope we do better next time.\"This story was updated to add more information.",
